1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
And now for you, Scoutmasters.

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,440
Hello, welcome to the Scoutmasters podcast.

3
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:13,200
This is a test for you.

4
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,800
This is a test that Green Bar Bill came up with in the 1930s,

5
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:20,760
in the edition of the Scoutmasters handbook.

6
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:22,320
I think 1939.

7
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:25,500
This is exactly what he wrote.

8
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:29,600
Get an easy chair and place it in a corner of the troop meeting room

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:37,560
if you, the Scoutmaster, can sink into that chair just after the opening ceremony

10
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:42,480
and just sit throughout the meeting without a worry for its success,

11
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:46,680
without lifting a finger or moving a foot until time comes for the closing,

12
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:50,200
well then your troop is run on the patrol method

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:52,940
and your boy leaders are actually leading.

14
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:54,580
I like it.

15
00:00:55,380 --> 00:00:56,060
Try it.

16
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,140
Let me know if you passed the test.

17
00:00:58,140 --> 00:01:01,840
Hey, this is podcast number 8.

18
00:01:28,140 --> 00:01:35,420
Hey, welcome back. Good to have you.

19
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:39,720
I want to say I appreciate all the feedback I've been getting through emails,

20
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:43,140
through ratings on iTunes, comments on the blog.

21
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,660
Certainly do appreciate it, and on Facebook.

22
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,600
This time around, well, I went a little long this time around,

23
00:01:50,680 --> 00:01:51,940
but I hope you'll think it's worth it.

24
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,600
In Scoutmastership, in seven minutes or less,

25
00:01:54,600 --> 00:01:59,899
we're going to talk a little bit about ten things that adult leaders do

26
00:01:59,899 --> 00:02:02,039
that can be frustrating to your youth leadership.

27
00:02:02,419 --> 00:02:06,220
I'm speaking from experience because I've frustrated many a youth leader in my day.

28
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,979
Next, I have a little story about probably the best 4th of July I ever spent at summer camp.

29
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:14,380
I think you'll get a kick out of it. I know I did.

30
00:02:14,700 --> 00:02:18,000
We're going to wind up with our theory in the series of moving a troop

31
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,840
that is being led by adults to a troop that is being led by youth.

32
00:02:22,359 --> 00:02:24,040
So let's get moving.

33
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,660
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

34
00:02:36,940 --> 00:02:39,840
You know, I feel like sometimes I get a little bit preachy,

35
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:43,160
and I want you to understand that the next few minutes,

36
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,120
I'm going to be talking about things that I have done,

37
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:48,739
and that unfortunately sometimes I continue to do

38
00:02:48,740 --> 00:02:55,680
because I'm as much of a learning curve as my scouts are in trying to figure this all out.

39
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:59,820
So this is not a missive from an ivory tower somewhere.

40
00:03:00,360 --> 00:03:05,240
This is just my experience and things that I have learned,

41
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,460
sometimes with great difficulty over time.

42
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:13,540
But I want to talk a little bit about 10 things that we do

43
00:03:13,540 --> 00:03:16,200
that can really be frustrating to youth leadership.

44
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:19,760
One of them is talking too much.

45
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:23,940
And this is a guy with a podcast and with a blog,

46
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:25,160
and I love to talk.

47
00:03:25,519 --> 00:03:28,060
I can talk the ears off of a donkey.

48
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:30,959
You know, I can talk the horns off a billy goat.

49
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,079
I can talk the feet off an iron pot.

50
00:03:33,459 --> 00:03:35,040
I am a good talker.

51
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:40,739
But when it's time to talk in the role of being a scoutmaster,

52
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:45,060
we've got to try and keep it short, direct, and intelligible.

53
00:03:45,299 --> 00:03:49,160
Scouts appreciate brevity and concision in your speech.

54
00:03:50,660 --> 00:03:55,220
You know, when they start to show an unusual interest in the ceiling or their shoes,

55
00:03:55,399 --> 00:03:57,120
you've talked for too long.

56
00:03:57,780 --> 00:03:59,920
I can't tell you exactly what that time is,

57
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:04,519
but it's somewhere in the two or three minute category.

58
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:08,019
That's why we have what we call a scoutmaster's minute.

59
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:10,679
Somebody was really smart when they came up with that

60
00:04:10,679 --> 00:04:14,000
because they know that none of us can.

61
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,500
And now I'm talking too long about talking too long.

62
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:19,719
All right, let's move on to number two, shall we?

63
00:04:20,099 --> 00:04:20,599
Coercion.

64
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,980
Adults can manipulate circumstances and conversations to bring about the result.

65
00:04:26,719 --> 00:04:29,219
And narrowly defining all the options and the ideas

66
00:04:29,219 --> 00:04:31,719
can be really unfairly coercive.

67
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:36,640
We have to look for direction from honest discussion with our scouts.

68
00:04:36,940 --> 00:04:42,580
Scouts come up with some very interesting and simple resolutions to difficult problems

69
00:04:42,580 --> 00:04:44,880
when we give them the latitude to do so.

70
00:04:45,240 --> 00:04:47,940
We don't need to coerce them into doing the right thing.

71
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:51,060
We need to help them discover the right thing

72
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:53,860
by letting them figure it out on their own.

73
00:04:54,560 --> 00:04:55,720
It really works.

74
00:04:56,020 --> 00:04:56,780
It really does.

75
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:58,120
Number three is nitpicking.

76
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:02,500
If it was up to me, I have a certain way that I would like a campsite set up.

77
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:05,680
I have a certain way that I would like everybody to do everything.

78
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:09,500
Because, you know, I can be a real nitpicker.

79
00:05:09,920 --> 00:05:13,180
But I'm learning not to stress the small stuff,

80
00:05:13,360 --> 00:05:15,320
not to concentrate on the small stuff,

81
00:05:15,380 --> 00:05:17,340
and to look for the good things that they do

82
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:20,440
and minimize the shortcomings they have sometimes

83
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,000
and celebrate their achievements.

84
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:24,360
So I try not to be a nitpicker.

85
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,940
Number four is right in there with nitpicking,

86
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:28,840
and it's micromanaging every little thing.

87
00:05:29,320 --> 00:05:32,500
Get a map out, show them the point on the map,

88
00:05:32,780 --> 00:05:34,940
and don't tell them how to take every step.

89
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,840
Allow them to discover the talents and abilities

90
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:40,240
without having to stop every ten minutes

91
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,880
to, you know, give them a minor course correction.

92
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:44,480
They're going to get there,

93
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:47,940
but they're going to get there in their way rather than yours.

94
00:05:48,340 --> 00:05:50,140
Number five, this can be a big one.

95
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,720
It's been a big one for me in the past, is anger.

96
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:55,200
Now, Thomas Jefferson said,

97
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:57,960
when you get angry, you count to ten before you speak.

98
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:00,300
If you're very angry, count to a hundred.

99
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:03,500
It's a dark storm. It'll pass.

100
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,220
You're going to be angry at one point,

101
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:08,320
especially if it's your son.

102
00:06:09,380 --> 00:06:11,560
I mean, frankly, it's going to make you angry.

103
00:06:13,440 --> 00:06:14,940
And counting to ten,

104
00:06:15,200 --> 00:06:18,040
well, maybe that's talking to one of the other adults first.

105
00:06:18,740 --> 00:06:21,100
Maybe it's just taking a little walk.

106
00:06:21,100 --> 00:06:29,500
It's irresponsible and childish to unload a big, angry load of stuff on scouts.

107
00:06:30,760 --> 00:06:31,720
They're learning,

108
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:37,000
and they need to have the latitude and the freedom to learn

109
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:41,240
without being afraid that somebody is going to just unload on them.

110
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:48,400
Number six is sacrificing the perfectly good for the perfect.

111
00:06:49,600 --> 00:06:51,460
Perfection is not our goal in scouts.

112
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:53,540
Remember you growing up?

113
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:55,480
Remember learning how to do things?

114
00:06:55,540 --> 00:06:57,000
It's messy and unpredictable.

115
00:06:58,000 --> 00:06:59,620
Let's not expect perfection.

116
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:01,280
Let's expect action.

117
00:07:01,640 --> 00:07:03,540
Let's expect moving towards the goal.

118
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:06,040
Number seven,

119
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:08,900
and this is a good one,

120
00:07:09,100 --> 00:07:13,400
especially when you're dealing with boys in those adolescent years.

121
00:07:14,080 --> 00:07:14,780
Number seven,

122
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:18,100
a good way to frustrate boys is by treating honest mistakes

123
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:19,820
as some kind of horrible crime.

124
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:21,940
Mistakes are inevitable.

125
00:07:22,360 --> 00:07:23,120
They're desirable.

126
00:07:23,560 --> 00:07:25,660
You want your scouts to make mistakes.

127
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:29,760
They indicate that they're taking action and that they're growing.

128
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:31,260
Really,

129
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:33,520
very few of your problems as a scoutmaster

130
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,000
are going to be from deliberate,

131
00:07:35,220 --> 00:07:37,620
premeditated disobedience or carelessness.

132
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:41,220
Scouts are going to make mistakes in judgment and action

133
00:07:41,220 --> 00:07:43,560
and seize on that moment

134
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:45,680
to get on their side

135
00:07:45,680 --> 00:07:47,820
and to be a little introspective

136
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:49,560
and to help them figure out what happened

137
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:51,480
and not to punish them.

138
00:07:51,980 --> 00:07:52,980
Number eight is hovering.

139
00:07:53,760 --> 00:07:55,100
You heard of helicopter parents?

140
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:56,820
How about helicopter scoutmasters?

141
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:57,880
You know,

142
00:07:58,520 --> 00:07:59,780
scouts need a lot of space.

143
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:01,920
They need a lot of real responsibility

144
00:08:01,920 --> 00:08:04,000
and they need a lot of autonomy.

145
00:08:04,920 --> 00:08:07,360
They don't need somebody breathing down their necks.

146
00:08:08,780 --> 00:08:08,960
Now,

147
00:08:09,020 --> 00:08:11,200
you have to maintain safety and propriety,

148
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:13,540
but you can do it from a respectful distance.

149
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:15,200
Let them play the game.

150
00:08:16,440 --> 00:08:17,200
Number nine

151
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,660
is shifting standards.

152
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:23,120
Don't change the rules in the middle of the game

153
00:08:23,120 --> 00:08:24,520
or otherwise,

154
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:25,020
you know,

155
00:08:25,120 --> 00:08:27,560
unfairly shift some kind of fundamental goal.

156
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:30,900
Every course needs some adjustment,

157
00:08:31,200 --> 00:08:32,659
but no one benefits from

158
00:08:32,659 --> 00:08:34,939
just continuously shifting

159
00:08:34,939 --> 00:08:36,520
the standard around

160
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:38,360
and then saying that

161
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:39,760
they didn't achieve something

162
00:08:39,760 --> 00:08:41,500
when they actually did.

163
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:44,100
Usurpation

164
00:08:44,100 --> 00:08:45,380
is number ten.

165
00:08:45,740 --> 00:08:48,100
How about that for a nine dollar college word, eh?

166
00:08:48,100 --> 00:08:49,100
Usurpation.

167
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:49,700
Usurpation.

168
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:51,740
Actual responsibility

169
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:53,400
in a scout troop

170
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:54,220
for behavior,

171
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:55,700
for achievement,

172
00:08:56,280 --> 00:08:57,320
and for action

173
00:08:57,320 --> 00:08:59,680
leads to actual achievement

174
00:08:59,680 --> 00:09:01,440
or actual disappointment.

175
00:09:02,140 --> 00:09:02,480
If

176
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:04,180
the adult leadership

177
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,740
usurps this responsibility

178
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:08,980
to make things easier

179
00:09:08,980 --> 00:09:10,200
or more predictable,

180
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:12,240
they compromise

181
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:13,340
the achievement.

182
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:15,340
actual responsibility

183
00:09:15,340 --> 00:09:17,100
for behavior

184
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:17,940
and achievement

185
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:18,720
and action

186
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:20,760
leads to actual achievement

187
00:09:20,760 --> 00:09:23,180
and that's why they need to have

188
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:25,440
real responsibility.

189
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:29,580
This has to be the truth, folks,

190
00:09:29,680 --> 00:09:32,320
because there is no way

191
00:09:32,320 --> 00:09:35,740
anyone could make this up.

192
00:09:35,740 --> 00:09:41,320
A couple decades ago,

193
00:09:41,860 --> 00:09:43,060
when I was on camp staff,

194
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:43,800
we used to be able

195
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:44,700
to buy fireworks

196
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:46,180
and put them off

197
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:47,260
for the 4th of July

198
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:48,700
when the scouts were in camp

199
00:09:48,700 --> 00:09:49,600
for the 4th of July.

200
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:51,640
Well, you can't do that anymore

201
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:52,400
and it's probably

202
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:53,120
a good thing, too.

203
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:55,240
There are a couple

204
00:09:55,240 --> 00:09:56,280
near misses there

205
00:09:56,280 --> 00:09:57,760
that, yeah,

206
00:09:57,820 --> 00:09:58,760
I wouldn't want to relive.

207
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:00,860
So, no more fireworks

208
00:10:00,860 --> 00:10:01,720
and we muddled through

209
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:02,460
for a couple years

210
00:10:02,460 --> 00:10:03,140
with just normal

211
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:03,880
old campfires

212
00:10:03,880 --> 00:10:05,360
and then there was one year

213
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:06,200
that I was camp director

214
00:10:06,200 --> 00:10:07,300
that I did the best thing

215
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:08,140
I have ever done

216
00:10:08,140 --> 00:10:08,600
in scouting.

217
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:10,460
Oh, it was marvelous.

218
00:10:11,140 --> 00:10:12,440
If I do say so myself.

219
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:13,180
And I do.

220
00:10:14,380 --> 00:10:15,860
Late last night

221
00:10:15,860 --> 00:10:17,340
when we were all in bed

222
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:19,180
This old Mary hug

223
00:10:19,180 --> 00:10:20,500
I let her near her shed

224
00:10:20,500 --> 00:10:22,100
When the cow could get over

225
00:10:22,100 --> 00:10:23,620
She winked her eye and said

226
00:10:23,620 --> 00:10:24,680
Be a hot ton

227
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:26,100
The old town's the night

228
00:10:26,100 --> 00:10:27,200
Fire, fire, fire

229
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:28,640
Late last night

230
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:30,640
When we were all in bed

231
00:10:30,640 --> 00:10:32,880
I talked to my maintenance guys

232
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:33,280
and I said,

233
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:34,500
find everything in camp

234
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:35,520
that we need to burn

235
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:39,600
And we're going to put it down

236
00:10:39,600 --> 00:10:40,360
in a big pile

237
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:41,240
We have a big field

238
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:42,140
in our camp

239
00:10:42,140 --> 00:10:44,080
And so we're going to

240
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:45,360
we're going to have a bonfire

241
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:46,340
We're going to have a bonfire

242
00:10:46,340 --> 00:10:47,800
like nobody's ever seen before

243
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:49,820
And it's made the maintenance guys

244
00:10:49,820 --> 00:10:50,400
very excited

245
00:10:50,400 --> 00:10:51,540
And they got very active

246
00:10:51,540 --> 00:10:52,540
and they found piles

247
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:53,360
and piles of stuff

248
00:10:53,360 --> 00:10:54,280
And they piled up

249
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:55,640
about 15 feet tall

250
00:10:55,640 --> 00:10:56,860
this bonfire

251
00:10:56,860 --> 00:10:58,520
that was just gorgeous

252
00:10:58,520 --> 00:11:00,420
We had a big giant

253
00:11:00,420 --> 00:11:01,440
American flag

254
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:03,080
One of our scout leaders

255
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:04,160
who was in camp for the week

256
00:11:04,160 --> 00:11:05,980
built a bit of a framework

257
00:11:05,980 --> 00:11:07,360
and we hung the big giant

258
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:08,200
American flag

259
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:09,780
on this truck

260
00:11:09,780 --> 00:11:11,520
so that it would

261
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:13,400
totally obscure

262
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:14,380
the campfire

263
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:15,680
I mean this was a big flag

264
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:16,220
this was like

265
00:11:16,220 --> 00:11:18,020
a 20 by 30 foot flag

266
00:11:18,020 --> 00:11:21,400
And so now imagine this

267
00:11:21,400 --> 00:11:24,840
The giant campfire is set up

268
00:11:24,840 --> 00:11:26,600
and the truck is parked

269
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:27,960
in front of the campfire

270
00:11:27,960 --> 00:11:29,680
and the way that the scouts

271
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:30,600
come down into the field

272
00:11:30,600 --> 00:11:31,860
they can't see the campfire at all

273
00:11:31,860 --> 00:11:32,680
they just see the giant

274
00:11:32,680 --> 00:11:33,480
American flag

275
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:35,500
So we built a little tiny

276
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:36,620
